## The arsenal

### Showing the product

The highest-value group on a company site (`§11`), and the most neglected.

| Technique | What it is | Where it earns its place |
|---|---|---|
| Live embedded product | The real application, running, in an iframe or a sandbox | The strongest conversion asset that exists. Rung 0–1 on the friction ladder |
| Scripted interactive demo | A faithful clickable replica driven by a script, not the real backend | When the real product needs auth or data. Much cheaper to maintain than it looks, and far better than video |
| Annotated UI crop | Real screen, cropped to one job, with callouts | Explains a mechanism in one screen. Works in email and in a deck too |
| Sequenced UI reveal | Screens stepping through a workflow on scroll or a timer | Shows a flow without a video's weight. Ship a paused, stepped fallback |
| Before / after scrubber | Draggable divider between two real states | Instantly legible proof for anything visual: migrations, cleanups, redesigns |
| Live data from the real system | A number, a chart, or a status pulled from production | Turns a rank-1 claim into something the visitor watches be true. Needs a designed offline state |
| Synthetic-data replica | The real UI, populated with realistic, non-real records | The answer when the product is under NDA or the data is personal (`§25`) |
| Video of the real thing | A real screen recording, short, poster-first, click to play | Never autoplay with sound. Poster image in the shell, player loaded on interaction |

**Never fake a screen that implies a feature that does not exist** (`§6`). A mocked-up
UI is allowed only when labeled as a concept, and on a marketing site it almost never
should be.

### Rendering and GPU

| Technique | What it is | Where it earns its place |
|---|---|---|
| Raymarching + SDFs | Geometry as a distance function, marched per pixel | A hero form that could not be modeled. Infinite repetition, soft shadows, ambient occlusion free |
| Domain warping (fbm) | Noise fed into the coordinates of more noise | Organic movement that never visibly loops. Atmosphere, ink, smoke, marble |
| Metaballs / marching squares | Implicit blobs merging on a field | Anything that should feel alive rather than placed. Merging nav states, liquid identity |
| Reaction-diffusion | Two chemicals competing on a grid | Growth over time. Strong for biotech, research, and anything that compounds |
| Voronoi / Delaunay | Space partitioned by proximity | Territory, coverage maps, crystalline structure, a grid that is not a grid |
| GPGPU particles | Particle state in textures, stepped in a shader | Millions of points at 60fps. Data made physical: transactions, users, deliveries |
| Curl-noise flow fields | Divergence-free noise driving velocity | Fluid motion without a fluid solver. Networks, logistics, flow of anything |
| Instanced geometry at 10k+ | One mesh, many transforms, one draw call | Scale as a statement. A field of the thing the client makes |
| Feedback loops (ping-pong FBO) | A buffer reading its own previous frame | Trails, decay, persistence. The cheapest route to a signature look |
| Caustics | Refracted light on a surface | Sells a scene as physical rather than drawn |
| MSDF text in WebGL | Signed-distance glyph atlases | Type inside a 3D scene, at any scale, still crisp |
| Procedural terrain | Mesh displaced by noise or real data | Topography, coverage, real geographic data as relief |
| WebGPU compute + WebGL fallback | Compute where available, degrade cleanly | Simulation budgets WebGL cannot reach. Only with the fallback built |

### Treatment and post-processing

Where `§10`-style discipline pays off: generated imagery gets *treated* in-browser
rather than placed raw, which is what makes it belong to this design instead of to a
model.

| Technique | What it is | Where it earns its place |
|---|---|---|
| Bloom | Bright regions bleeding into neighbors | Makes emissive things read as light rather than pale color |
| Film grain | Animated noise over the composite | Kills banding, unifies CSS and canvas layers, adds material to flat gradients |
| Displacement | One texture pushing another's UVs | Hover distortion, transitions, heat, liquid. The workhorse |
| LUT grading | A 3D lookup applied to the final frame | One pass that makes every layer share a grade. The difference between colored and art-directed |
| Duotone / posterize / halftone | Quantized tone with structure | Pulls stock-feeling or generated imagery into the brand palette. Cheap, and instantly proprietary |
| Ordered dithering | Structured quantization | Print, plotter, early-computing registers. Compresses beautifully |
| Depth-map parallax | A flat frame plus a depth map, displaced on pointer | Turns one generated image into a scene with volume. High payoff per byte |
| Chromatic aberration | Channel separation at edges | Lens realism at rest, energy under motion. Scale with scroll velocity |
| Edge detection / sobel | Gradient magnitude as outline | Blueprint and diagram registers. Pairs with posterize |
| Vignette | Falloff toward the frame | Directs the eye. Invisible when right, obvious when missing |
| Motion blur | Accumulated or velocity-buffer smear | Speed that reads as speed rather than strobe |

### CSS and SVG native

The cheapest wins in the file. Most of this costs nothing in bytes and nothing in
main-thread time, which makes it the right first reach on a site with a performance
budget (`§19`).

| Technique | What it is | Where it earns its place |
|---|---|---|
| OKLCH color and gradients | Perceptual color space | Gradients with no gray dead zone. Use it everywhere and the palette improves for free |
| `@property` | Typed, animatable custom properties | Gradients, angles, and colors that actually interpolate. CSS-only effects that used to need JS |
| `animation-timeline` | Native scroll-driven animation | Scroll effects off the main thread with no library. Check support, fall back to a library |
| View Transitions API | Native cross-document and same-document morphs | Continuity between pages. A pricing tier that becomes the signup form |
| Container queries | Components responding to their own box | Real art direction per component. Correct for a card living in three contexts |
| CSS anchor positioning | Elements tethered to other elements | Annotations, tooltips, and marginalia without JS measurement |
| SVG filters | `feTurbulence`, `feDisplacementMap`, `feColorMatrix` | Procedural noise, warping, and duotone on live DOM. Text and images, no canvas |
| The gooey filter | Blur into contrast matrix | Metaball merging on real HTML elements. Nav items that fuse |
| Masks and clip-paths | Alpha, luminance, and geometric clipping | Non-rectangular layout. The fastest exit from the card grid |
| `mix-blend-mode` | Compositing between stacked layers | Type that reacts to what passes behind it. Inverting cursors |
| `backdrop-filter` | Filtering what sits behind an element | Real depth in sticky UI and nav |
| Conic / repeating gradients | Gradient functions as pattern | Texture and moiré at zero byte cost |
| Morphing SVG paths | Interpolation between path definitions | A logo that transforms into the product's core shape |
| `text-wrap: balance` / `pretty` | Native line-break optimization | Headlines that break where a designer would. One declaration |
| `:has()` | Parent and sibling selection | State-driven layout with no JS. Form states, filled-field styling, section adaptation |
| Houdini paint worklets | Custom paint in a background image | Procedural texture inside CSS layout. Narrow support, always with a fallback |

### Motion and input

| Technique | What it is | Where it earns its place |
|---|---|---|
| Scrubbed scroll timelines | Progress tied to scroll position | Scroll as a scrub head. Use for one section, never a whole marketing page |
| Spring and inertia physics | Motion resolved by simulation | Interfaces that respond to how the input arrived |
| Magnetic elements | Targets attracted to the pointer | Weighted feel on the primary CTA. A few elements only |
| Custom cursors | The pointer replaced or augmented | Signals a designed environment within a second. Desktop only — design mobile first |
| Variable fonts on all axes | Scroll or pointer driving every axis | Optical size, width, grade, slant. **Weight alone is the amateur signal** — read the font's axis list |
| Per-glyph transforms | Individual characters in 3D | Headlines that assemble and settle. High effort, high recall |
| Split-text stagger | Sequencing at glyph or word level | Entrances with rhythm. The enabler for most of this group |
| Scroll-velocity distortion | Scroll speed feeding a shader or filter | The site responds to how fast someone moves. Subtle, immediately felt |
| Cursor-as-light-source | Pointer driving a mask or light position | Reveals and spotlights the visitor navigates by hand |
| Pointer-tilt / device orientation | Gyroscope driving parallax | Depth on mobile where there is no hover. Permission-aware fallback |
| Number roll-ups on reveal | Counting to a real figure | Only for rank-1 numbers, and only once per page. Otherwise it is a tic |

### Whole-page moves

| Technique | What it is | Where it earns its place |
|---|---|---|
| Designed preloader | A composed hold, not a spinner | Only when there is genuinely something to wait for. Never in front of a static page |
| Cross-document View Transitions | Page-to-page continuity | A multi-page site that feels like one surface, with no framework |
| Progressive disclosure | Depth revealed on demand | The committee problem: a skimmable page that a deep reader can open up |
| Sticky section pinning | A section held while its content advances | One per page, maximum. Two is a tunnel |
| Scroll-linked nav state | Nav adapting to position and direction | Keeps the CTA reachable without a permanent bar eating the viewport |
| Print stylesheet | A real one | Procurement prints pricing pages. It costs 30 lines and someone will thank you |
| Reduced-data mode | `prefers-reduced-data` respected | Drop the heavy layer entirely. Cheap, and honest |

